# local.conf.example - the settings of one machine, each with its default and what it does.
#
# How to use it
#   cd ~/gentoo-laptop
#   cp local.conf.example local.conf     once; git ignores local.conf, so updates never touch it
#   (edit local.conf: remove the "#" in front of a setting and change its value)
#   sudo ./deploy                        shows the change, asks, and installs it as
#                                        /etc/gentoo-laptop/local.conf, where the scripts read it
# Then do what "Takes effect" says in the setting's section. To go back to a default, put the "#"
# back (or delete the line) and deploy again. Deleting local.conf and deploying removes the
# installed copy, and every setting is at its default again.
#
# The format
#   One setting per line: NAME=VALUE - no spaces around "=", no quotes, no spaces in the value.
#   A comment may follow the value after a space:    ZRAM_PERCENT=40   # less for this machine
#   Lines starting with "#" and empty lines are ignored. A name set twice: the last line counts.
#   An empty value (NAME=) means the default.
# deploy checks every line before it installs anything: a name it does not know (a typo) or a
# value of the wrong form stops it, with the line number. The allowed values come from the scripts
# themselves, so this file and the scripts cannot disagree. A script that still finds a bad value
# (local.conf edited in /etc by hand) says so and uses the default.
#
# Each machine keeps its own local.conf, so two laptops can differ. It lives in your home, so the
# home's snapshots and backups keep it. install-gentoo.sh takes it too when you put one in the
# folder you run it from: the new system then starts with these settings.

# --- Battery charge limits (ThinkPad: /usr/local/sbin/battery-limits) -----------------------------
# The firmware stops charging at the END value and starts again only once the battery is below the
# START value. On the dock (any Thunderbolt device connected) the dock limits apply: a laptop that
# lives plugged in ages more slowly kept below full. Away from the dock, and after
# "sudo battery-limits full" (before a trip), the away limits apply. START must be below END:
# otherwise that pair falls back to its defaults, and battery-limits says so. Set both of a pair
# when you change one. Laptops without these firmware controls ignore all four.
# Takes effect: at once with "sudo battery-limits"; it also runs at each boot, dock and undock.
# "battery-limits status" shows the place, the limits in force and the battery.
# Docked: charging starts again when the battery is below this, in percent (0-99).
#BATTERY_DOCK_START=75
# Docked: charging stops at this, in percent (1-100).
#BATTERY_DOCK_END=80
# Away from the dock: charging starts again below this (0-99); 99 tops the battery up almost always.
#BATTERY_AWAY_START=99
# Away from the dock: charging stops at this (1-100); 100 is a full battery.
#BATTERY_AWAY_END=100

# --- Compressed swap in RAM (zram: /etc/local.d/zram.start) ---------------------------------------
# With 16 GB of RAM or more, memory nobody has touched for a while is compressed into zram, in RAM,
# instead of being written to a disk. (Under 16 GB the installer makes an encrypted swap partition
# with zswap in front of it instead - the ZSWAP_ settings below; zram is then not used at all.)
# Takes effect: at the next boot. Or now, when memory is not tight - this moves what zram holds
# back into RAM first:  sudo /etc/local.d/zram.stop && sudo /etc/local.d/zram.start
# "zramctl" shows the size (DISKSIZE), what it holds (DATA) and what that takes in RAM (TOTAL).
# How much zram may hold, uncompressed, in percent of the RAM (1-100). This is a ceiling, not a
# reservation: zram takes RAM only for what it holds, compressed to about a third, plus a table of
# about half a percent of the size. More lets virtual machines and other big workloads go past the
# RAM without being killed; the cost is that a runaway program also takes longer to be stopped.
#ZRAM_PERCENT=50
# The compression: zstd packs tightest; lz4 is faster and packs less; lzo-rle is the kernel's own
# default. "cat /sys/block/zram0/comp_algorithm" lists what this kernel offers (the one in use in
# brackets). One it does not offer leaves the kernel's default, and zram.start says so at boot.
#ZRAM_COMPRESSOR=zstd

# --- Compressed cache before a swap partition (zswap: /etc/local.d/zswap.start) -------------------
# Only on a machine with a swap partition - the installer makes one under 16 GB of RAM; elsewhere
# these do nothing. Memory on its way to the partition is compressed and kept in RAM first; what
# nobody touches then moves on to the partition.
# Takes effect: at the next boot, or now with:  sudo /etc/local.d/zswap.start
# "grep -r . /sys/module/zswap/parameters" shows zswap's state.
# The compression, as for zram: zstd (tightest), lz4 (faster, packs less), lzo (the kernel's
# default). One the kernel does not offer keeps the one in use, and zswap.start says so.
#ZSWAP_COMPRESSOR=zstd
# The most of the RAM zswap may fill with compressed memory, in percent (1-100). Past it, the
# oldest of it goes to the swap partition.
#ZSWAP_POOL_PERCENT=25

# --- Process history (atop: /etc/default/atop) -----------------------------------------------------
# atop records every process - CPU, memory, disk - so that after the desktop stalled you can see
# which program it was:  atop -r /var/log/atop/atop_YYYYMMDD -b HH:MM
# Takes effect: with the next day's file (at midnight), or now with:  sudo rc-service atop restart
# Seconds between two samples (1 or more). Shorter catches briefer episodes and takes more disk:
# every 60 s is about 13 MiB a day at 450 processes.
#ATOP_INTERVAL=60
# Days of history kept (1 or more): one file a day in /var/log/atop; older ones are deleted at
# midnight.
#ATOP_DAYS=28

# --- Windows back in place after re-docking (redock-watch, in the desktop session) ----------------
# After an undock it moves windows from the vanished monitors onto the laptop's screen, and after a
# re-dock it puts them back where they were.
# Takes effect: at the next login.
# Seconds without window changes before it notes where every window is (decimals allowed: 0.5).
#REDOCK_QUIET=1
# Seconds after the last monitor change before it acts on an undock or a re-dock (decimals
# allowed). Raise it - try 0.5 to 2 - if windows land in the wrong place because the monitors were
# still settling.
#REDOCK_SETTLE=1

# --- Memory cap of the desktop session (session-memory-limit, at login) ---------------------------
# Everything started from the desktop shares one soft cap: past it, the session is slowed down and
# its memory pushed into compressed swap, instead of the whole machine stalling. A text console
# (Ctrl+Alt+F2) and SSH logins are outside it and stay quick. Nothing is killed.
# Takes effect: at the next login.
# The cap, in one of three forms: a share of the RAM, 1 to 100 % with decimals allowed (50%,
# 62.5%); a size with its unit K, M, G or T (24G - a bare number would be bytes, so it is refused);
# or max for no cap at all. Empty (the default): 62.5%, 5/8 of the RAM - 20 GiB with 32 GB. Wins
# over MEMORY_HIGH in /etc/session-memory-limit.conf, the older place for this.
#SESSION_MEMORY_HIGH=

# --- Wi-Fi hotspot for your own devices (sudo hotspot start: /usr/local/sbin/hotspot) -------------
# Shares the laptop's connection - a hotel's Wi-Fi after its sign-in page, or a cable - with your
# phone and the family's over a WPA2 network of the laptop's own, while the laptop stays connected.
# From the hotspot, devices reach the internet, and of the laptop only addresses, DNS and ping.
# Takes effect: at the next "sudo hotspot start" (or "sudo hotspot restart" while it runs).
# "sudo hotspot status" shows the name and passphrase in use; "sudo hotspot qr" a QR code for phones.
# The network's name (1-32 letters, digits, . _ -). Empty (the default): AndroidAP_ and four
# digits, made up once and kept - the name stock Android phones give their own hotspot, so it
# looks like one more phone among the many in a hotel. Spaces are not possible here.
#HOTSPOT_SSID=
# The passphrase (8-63 of letters, digits and . _ ~ ! @ $ % ^ & * ( ) + = , : ; ? / -). Empty (the
# default): 16 letters and digits, made up once and kept, so devices that joined once rejoin.
# It sits in local.conf in clear text, readable by every account on this laptop: fine for a
# hotspot key you hand to family, not for a password that matters elsewhere.
#HOTSPOT_PASSPHRASE=
# The band: bg is 2.4 GHz - every device, longer reach, allowed everywhere without a country set;
# a is 5 GHz - faster, but only once the country allows an access point there (iw reg get shows
# it; the hotel's network usually tells the laptop its country after you connect).
#HOTSPOT_BAND=bg
# The channel; 0 (the default): the one the laptop's own Wi-Fi is on when that is in the band -
# one channel for the radio is the fastest - else 6 (2.4 GHz) or 36 (5 GHz).
#HOTSPOT_CHANNEL=0

# --- Not here ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Whole configuration files are still changed in files/ and deployed: the snapshot schedule
# (files/sanoid.conf), SSH (files/sshd-keys-only.conf), ZFSBootMenu (files/zfsbootmenu-config.yaml)
# and the initramfs (files/dracut-zfs.conf). The ZFS read cache is set once by the installer, in
# /etc/modprobe.d/zfs.conf; the build jobs in /etc/portage/make.conf. Before an install,
# install-gentoo.sh's own settings (disk, swap partition, cache size) are at its top.
